Z||000000||drednot||04-20-2001||09:20 PM||||Well, my last suspension job lasted about a month.  Something else must have gone bad.  My last suspension job, I got a new rack installed which included new tie rod ends.  That cleared up the ride a bit, but it was still not as nice as my dads car (basically a very gussied up Honda).  It is starting to got bad again!  It is drifting (again) when it gets up into triple digit speeds.  When it hits small pot holes, it makes a clunking (almost a rattling) sound.  I have rattles coming from the front passenger side wheel, and the rear passenger side wheel.  My rear tire is also wearing unevenly.  I an not the nicest person in the world to the car, but I don't drive like I stole the thing (except on Friday and Saturday nights + I have been known to catch some air sometimes).  What could this be now???  I am getting tired of the same old problems, and  I just want the suspension fixed!  My budget for getting this fixed is $500.  If I can't get it fixed for that, Anyone want a used Fiero in need of a suspension overhaul?||||reg||8
Z||000001||WKDFIRO||04-20-2001||11:23 PM||||Does your dad "catch air" in his Honda?<P>Maybe thats why his suspension is so nice.||||reg||5
Z||000002||theogre||04-21-2001||12:23 AM||||Quote-&gt;I have been known to catch some air sometimes....<P>Quote-&gt;Something else must have gone bad.<P>Quote-&gt;It is drifting (again) when it gets up into triple digit speeds.<P>Do you really think any sane person will feel sorry for you. Of course the car is broken again. It's not an Abrams Tank... It's not the Space Shuttle... It's not a Subaru WRX... Duh. Just Maybe it wasn't built to drive like that. This is the same kind of logic slammers have when the ball joints split from having no suspension in the car..... There's not a whole lot of things funnier than seeing a slammed nimrod sitting in the middle of the parkway with the front wheels pointing in 2 diferant directions. Even if they did back traffic up nearly to JFK....<P>If you want a car to drive like a fool then get something other than a Fiero. You will never be happy with one. You could spend $5000 on the suspension and never get it to hold up to what you are doing.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Spektyr||04-21-2001||01:07 AM||||I used to laugh at people that slowed down for railroad tracks as I serenely glided over them at 40mph in my Lincoln Town car (or my Nissan PU with offroad shocks before that).<P>Now I'm the guy going "Was that a DIP sign?" as I stand on the brakes.<P>Sure, I'll hammer it into corners, burn the hides off it and generally drive the hell out of it.<P>But if the road is rough, you can bet I'm at least gear braking.  Performance doesn't mean indestructable.  It means that the envelope isn't a suggestion.<P>How long do you think a race-ready motor lasts above redline?  Not very long.  A stock Detroit motor?  Longer than you'd think.  (Ask pontiac4me, he helped his buddy blow a junker's motor sky high, hehe).<P>Why?  Because the redline on the race motor isn't a suggestion.  The parts are built to exacting tolerances, and it's built to be driven by a professional.  The Detroit motor on the other hand is designed to forgive little mistakes, and the 'redline' on it is posted below the actual limit of the motor.  That's to keep the average person from turning their motor into a silly-looking paperweight the first time they screw up and over-rev it.<P>So what's this have to do with suspension?  Basically this: use things the way they are designed and they last longer.  If you take your mom's Chevy Celebrity out four-wheeling, you end up with a very expensive tow/repair bill.  Your average car can handle it's share of potholes, curb checks, and even a minor Dukes of Hazard moment or two.<P>The Fiero isn't average.  It wasn't designed to get you, the wife and your 2.5 kids from point A to point B.  It was designed to perform.  It was designed to go AROUND the bumps so nimbly that your passenger goes white-knuckled on the arm rest.  It needs an alert driver who's aware of what the car can and can't do.<P>Cars (and especially sports cars) are referred to as female for a reason.  If you treat them right, fulfill their needs and give them the attention they deserve they will do everything in their power to make you happy.  If you mistreat them, they get tempermental, bitchy and eventually stop doing anything for you at all.<P>From a purely practical standpoint, remember this:  Maintenance always costs less than repairs.<P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)||||reg||1
Z||000004||FLASHY||04-21-2001||01:18 AM||||Hey, Voytek has been having that same "clunk" you are talking about. He says it's coming from the front somewhere around that area. I know he's going to try having a look at it to see what's wrong this weekend. He'll post something on Monday if he finds anything I'm sure. If you find out what's wrong, I know he'll be interested to know.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Fierowrecker||04-21-2001||07:49 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by drednot:<BR><B>Well, my last suspension job lasted about a month.  Something else must have gone bad.  My last suspension job, I got a new rack installed which included new tie rod ends.  That cleared up the ride a bit, but it was still not as nice as my dads car (basically a very gussied up Honda).  It is starting to got bad again!  It is drifting (again) when it gets up into triple digit speeds.  When it hits small pot holes, it makes a clunking (almost a rattling) sound.  I have rattles coming from the front passenger side wheel, and the rear passenger side wheel.  My rear tire is also wearing unevenly.  I an not the nicest person in the world to the car, but I don't drive like I stole the thing (except on Friday and Saturday nights + I have been known to catch some air sometimes).  What could this be now???  I am getting tired of the same old problems, and  I just want the suspension fixed!  My budget for getting this fixed is $500.  If I can't get it fixed for that, Anyone want a used Fiero in need of a suspension overhaul?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>If you are going to drive it like it is a race car, know that race cars get overhauled before EVERY race...<BR>When you throw 3000 pounds into the air and it lands, it can flex the chassis, and put things out of alignment...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
